When generating git commit messages, follow Conventional Commits v1.0.0:
https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/

Required format:

<type>(<scope>): <description>

`<filename>` - <what changed in this file>
`<filename>` - <what changed in this file>

Subject rules (per spec):
- type: feat (new feature), fix (bug fix), or build, chore, ci, docs, style, refactor, perf, test, revert
- optional scope: logical area (header, movies, ui, apps, projects, router, api)
- description: short summary after colon and space; imperative mood, lowercase, no trailing period
- breaking changes: append ! before : (e.g. feat(api)!: remove endpoint) or add BREAKING CHANGE: footer

Body rules (project convention on top of spec):
- Blank line after subject (required by spec)
- One line per materially changed staged file
- Format: `<filename>` - short explanation
- Filename only unless path needed to disambiguate
- Order by importance; skip lockfiles and generated artifacts unless main change
- Do not repeat the subject in the body

Example:
feat(header): update header links with current page

`HeaderLinks.tsx` - highlight active route and sync nav links with current page
`utils.ts` - add helper to resolve current page from pathname

Never commit .env, credentials, or secrets.
